---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import { paperTrailsCaseStudy } from '../../data/case-studies';

const sectionLinks = [
    { href: '#rationale', label: 'Why rebuild?' },
    { href: '#decision', label: 'The decision' },
    { href: '#scope', label: 'Scope and delivery' },
    { href: '#release', label: 'Release and compatibility' },
    { href: '#outcome', label: 'Outcome' },
    { href: '#lessons', label: 'What I learned' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout study={paperTrailsCaseStudy} sectionLinks={sectionLinks}>
    <Fragment slot="intro">
        I co-founded Paper Trails with my brother and look after everything technical. In late 2025, I rebuilt
        our React frontend and PHP API as one Next.js and TypeScript application. It took roughly three months
        of part-time work alongside my full-time job. We went live in early January 2026, ahead of our
        February target.
    </Fragment>
    <dl slot="facts" class="facts">
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Technical Director and Co-Founder</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Migration</dt>
            <dd>React / PHP API to Next.js / TypeScript</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Launch</dt>
            <dd>Early January 2026</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
            <dt>Transition</dt>
            <dd>Less than one hour of downtime</dd>
        </div>
    </dl>
    <section class="section" id="rationale">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">01 / Why rebuild?</p>
            <h2>The old app was getting in the way</h2>
            <p>
                Paper Trails turns outdoor activity data into personalised prints. The product worked, but
                maintaining it was becoming awkward. The frontend and PHP backend lived in separate
                repositories, small bugs were accumulating, and I had to keep both applications in step.
            </p>
            <p>
                Partner campaigns made the problem more obvious. Adding a custom theme meant changing lots of
                files, then checking and releasing those changes across both apps. That was too much work for
                something we'd need to do again.
            </p>
            <p>
                My brother and I went through analytics, order history and database logs to see what people
                actually used. We wanted to make custom work and maintenance easier while keeping the ordering
                flow and access to old print images working. A possible partner campaign gave us a February
                2026 target.
            </p>
            <p>
                I handled the architecture, implementation and delivery planning. My brother worked on
                branding, marketing and theme design. We agreed together what to keep and what to leave out.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="decision">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">02 / The decision</p>
            <h2>Why I chose a rewrite</h2>
            <p>
                I did consider refactoring the PHP application. Replacing a working product meant spending
                time on a rewrite, changing the deployment setup and risking new bugs. But I'd over-engineered
                the original system. Untangling it would still leave me with two applications in different
                languages to maintain.
            </p>
            <p>
                I'd already used Next.js at Quilter, so it was familiar. Bringing the client and server code
                together meant I could share TypeScript models and validation, and make changes in one place.
                Server actions replaced our separate REST API. The server still handled validation and
                third-party credentials, and we still needed the external integrations and webhooks.
            </p>
            <p>
                We kept much of the database structure and brought existing test cases across. We also moved
                checkout to Stripe's hosted payment flow, giving us less payment code to look after.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="scope">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">03 / Scope and delivery</p>
            <h2>Deciding what could wait</h2>
            <p>
                We agreed the must-haves before I started building. Customers sometimes ordered the preloaded
                example activities by mistake, so we removed them and started with an empty map. Automatic
                print labels also went. Differences in units and activity data had created too many edge cases
                and support questions.
            </p>
            <p>
                The old feature backlog could wait. Getting the replacement into use and checking that it
                worked mattered more than adding everything we'd thought of along the way.
            </p>
            <p>
                I wrote technical design docs for the main features and used them to break the work into
                smaller GitHub issues under parent issues. Labels, milestones and a Kanban board helped me
                pick things back up between other commitments.
            </p>
            <p>
                AI agents helped organise the work and wrote much of the implementation. They updated issues
                and supplied test results, screenshots and browser reproductions for me to review. I checked
                the customer experience myself, with Storybook helping me inspect themes and component
                variations.
            </p>
            <p>
                I started with tooling and tests, then worked through the application structure, print
                creation, checkout and integrations. Checking each feature as I went made several months of
                part-time work manageable.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="release">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">04 / Release and compatibility</p>
            <h2>Moving across without losing old orders</h2>
            <p>
                Before launch, I worked through a deployment checklist on staging and a production release
                candidate. I backed up the database, image storage and repositories, then used analytics to
                choose a quiet time to switch the domain.
            </p>
            <p>
                Moving the existing order data and images took less than an hour of downtime. Customers could
                still use their old order IDs to retrieve print images. Prefixes in those IDs let us tell the
                versions apart and return the right image.
            </p>
            <p>
                Once we'd switched the domain, we made a live payment ourselves and watched for errors. I kept
                the old application running with everything it needed to take orders. If the payment check
                failed or errors appeared, I could switch the domain back and investigate the logs.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="outcome">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">05 / Outcome</p>
            <h2>What changed</h2>
            <p>
                We launched in early January, ahead of the February target. A custom theme now involves only a
                few files, and shared models and validation mean less duplicated code.
            </p>
            <p>
                I also spend less time moving between repositories and coordinating releases. GitHub Actions
                runs the checks, and a merge to main deploys the whole application on DigitalOcean. I haven't
                put a number on the time saved, but there's less coordination involved in getting a change
                live.
            </p>
            <p>
                After launch, we added an order dashboard that brings payment, email and fulfilment events
                together. Better logging helped us track down memory growth during on-demand image processing
                too. We addressed that by generating optimised images ahead of deployment.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="lessons">
        <div class="section__inner">
            <p class="eyebrow">06 / What I learned</p>
            <h2>What I&#x27;d do differently</h2>
            <p>
                I'd built too much into the old system for things that never became important. Looking at
                actual usage helped us make better decisions about what deserved to stay.
            </p>
            <p>
                The other lesson was about how I checked the work. I'd broken it into small tasks, but I
                needed to follow smaller changes all the way through the ordering flow too.
            </p>
            <p>
                A post-launch review of our end-to-end tests showed gaps around the return from Stripe
                checkout. Unfinished print configurations lived in browser storage, so getting them back after
                payment depended on browser state. That was fragile and hard to test across browsers.
            </p>
            <p>
                I introduced temporary MongoDB records with a 72-hour expiry so we could retrieve the
                configuration independently of the browser. The test review prompted that change.
            </p>
            <p>
                Next time, I'd take something as specific as a print's orientation and check that it survived
                checkout and appeared correctly in the completed order. Then I'd expand to the other
                customisation options.
            </p>
            <p>
                For a closer look at a separate production fix, read the
                <a href="/case-studies/ai-engineering/">Paper Trails checkout case study</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
    .facts {
        display: grid;
        margin-top: 48px;
        border-top: 2px solid var(--site-border);
        border-left: 2px solid var(--site-border);
    }

    .fact {
        min-width: 0;
        padding: 20px;
        border-right: 2px solid var(--site-border);
        border-bottom: 2px solid var(--site-border);
    }
</style>
